#!/bin/bash
# land-routine-budget-ui.sh — the routine sheet learns to show its own money.
#
# Monthly cost and how long a pack lasts on every step; a FACE / HAIR / BODY
# strip above the routine with budget, cost, remaining and a calm bar; the
# short-budget ask; the "we don't recommend spending what's left" line; and the
# sentences saying what was deliberately left out.
#
# BOTH SIDES (together-city-chat + together-city-react). Vercel AND Railway.
# PRECONDITION: land-budget-key-collision.sh.
set -euo pipefail

cd "$(dirname "$0")"
[ -d together-city-chat ] || { echo "!! Run this from the Together-Ai repo root."; exit 1; }

if [ -f .git/index.lock ] && [ ! -s .git/index.lock ]; then
  echo "== clearing a stale empty .git/index.lock"
  rm -f .git/index.lock
fi

NEEDS="A budget you cannot see being spent"
LOG="$(git log --oneline -40)"
case "$LOG" in
  *"$NEEDS"*) echo "== \"$NEEDS\" is already here. Nothing to do."; exit 0 ;;
esac
case "$LOG" in
  *"A stored blob is not a type"*) ;;
  *) echo "!! Run land-budget-key-collision.sh first — this is written against the tree it produces."; exit 1 ;;
esac

ALLOWED='^ M together-city-react/(public/assets/img/(apple-touch-icon-180|tc-icon-1024|tc-icon-192|tc-icon-512|tc-icon-maskable-512)\.png|public/downloads/TogetherCity\.apk)$'
DIRTY="$(git status --porcelain \
  | grep -Ev '^\?\? (land-.*\.sh|push-.*\.sh|.*\.patch)$' \
  | grep -Ev "$ALLOWED" || true)"
if [ -n "$DIRTY" ]; then
  echo "!! The tree carries changes this script did not expect:"; echo "$DIRTY"; exit 1
fi

PATCH="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/land.XXXXXX")"
trap 'rm -f "$PATCH"' EXIT
cat <<'B64EOF' | tr -d '\n' | openssl base64 -d -A > "$PATCH"

B64EOF
WANT="cc0a86b0a02048c7776f0b95a6e9add88abb95c84f078fd82033c16351236dbf"
GOT="$(shasum -a 256 "$PATCH" | awk '{print $1}')"
[ "$GOT" = "$WANT" ] || { echo "!! Patch is corrupt."; echo "   want $WANT"; echo "   got  $GOT"; exit 1; }
echo "== patch verified"
git apply --check -C1 "$PATCH" || { echo "!! Patch does not apply cleanly. Nothing written."; exit 1; }
git apply -C1 "$PATCH"
echo "== applied"

echo "== gates: the API"
cd together-city-chat
npx tsc --noEmit
npx jest src/beauty
cd ..

echo "== gates: the web app"
cd together-city-react
npx tsc --noEmit
npx vitest run
node scripts/a11y-audit.mjs
node scripts/motion-ceiling.mjs
node scripts/lint-ceiling.mjs
npx vite build
cd ..

git add together-city-chat/src/beauty/budget-routine.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/beauty.service.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/budget-is-a-limit.spec.ts \
        together-city-react/src/features/beauty/api.ts \
        together-city-react/src/features/beauty/pages/Routine.tsx \
        together-city-react/src/styles/layout.css \
        together-city-react/src/app/budget-is-on-the-page.test.ts
git commit -F - <<'MSG'
A budget you cannot see being spent

The engine had been costing this routine by the month for a week and the page
said nothing about it. Four products with four purchase prices — 284, 569, 408,
474 — sitting under a MONTHLY limit of 5,000. Every number was true and the sum
of them answered a question nobody had asked, because a cleanser that lasts six
weeks and a serum that lasts eight months do not belong in the same total.

Every step now carries both figures and the working behind them: 474, then
"≈ 158/month", then "one 50 ml pack — about 3 months". That last sentence is
what makes the middle one believable; without it "≈ 158/month" is an assertion
about somebody's money that they have no way to check.

Above the routine, one card per category — face, hair and body, because that is
how the budget was set and how it is spent. Monthly budget, routine cost,
remaining, and a bar drawn in the hub's own accent and in nothing else. A budget
four-fifths spent is a budget working, not a warning, and the alarm tokens in
this hub are reserved for things that can hurt somebody's skin. The bar cannot
overflow because the planner never takes a product it cannot afford.

Three sentences the plan had been computing and nobody had ever read:

  · what was left out and why — "you don't need a separate toner" and "a
    treatment step would fit your profile but not this budget" are different
    facts, and a lean routine has to say which one it means;
  · the short budget, which asks rather than adjusts: Set 1,650, or Keep 1,000,
    both spelled out, and nothing moved on anybody's behalf;
  · the money left over, which is not a gap to fill. "You have 2,860 left, and
    we don't recommend adding products simply to use it."

Adjust budget is a link back to the profile, not a second set of dials. One
place, still.

And the plan is now serialised for the wire. It used to send the whole product
object inside every pick while this app's type declared an id — two files that
had never agreed, compiling happily, costing nothing only because the one field
the page read was `skipped`. The first join on productId would have rendered a
page with no monthly costs on it and thrown nothing anywhere. Same failure as
the budget-key collision, one layer up, and the same fix: assert the shape at
the boundary instead of trusting the annotation at either end.

Tests: the wire shape (id present, product object absent, money unchanged, a
zero category zero all the way out) and the page (the join, both prices, the
pack sentence, all three budget numbers, the accent-only bar, both doors on a
short budget, one place to set it).
MSG

echo "== committed"
cat <<'DONE'

===============================================================
 Landed: A budget you cannot see being spent
 Both sides — push and Vercel rebuilds the web app while
 Railway redeploys the API. The routine page needs both.
===============================================================

DONE
